\section{Design file format}{

The file is an \code{.xlsx} workbook with one sheet, \code{packages}, and one row per
package. The columns are, in order:
\itemize{
\item \code{atc}: the full ATC code.
\item \code{produkt}: the product name, as LMED writes it.
\item \code{lnmn}: the product name and strength, matched after
\code{tolower(trimws())}.
\item \code{pack_ddd}: the DDDs that one pack of this package holds. A
dispensing of \code{antal} packs has \code{fddd = antal * pack_ddd}. It MUST be
greater than 0.
\item \code{form}: the dose form, as free text, for example \code{"tablet"}.
\item \code{dur_min}, \code{dur_usual}, \code{dur_max}: durations of one pack, in
days. Each MUST be greater than 0, and
\verb{dur_min <= dur_usual <= dur_max} MUST hold.
\item \code{status}: \code{"auto"} or \code{"approved"}.
\item \code{source}, \code{note}: text. Both MAY be empty.
}
Every column except \code{source} and \code{note} MUST hold a value on every row.
Two rows with the same \code{atc}, \code{produkt} and normalised \code{lnmn} MUST have
values of \code{pack_ddd} that differ by 0.01 or more. One dispensing therefore
matches at most one row.

The reader stops on the first rule that a row breaks, and names every sheet
row that breaks it. Row 1 of the sheet is the header. The reader ignores
every column that is not in the list above or in the section "Design
dates". It does not check the column order.
